|~ `::set` and what is declared under it: its constructor and the
   refusal of a payload that is no vector, and the verbs whose answer on a
   set is not the answer of the verb of vectors on it. A set is the vector
   in the one order without duplicates, and every other verb reaches it
   through the walk as the verb of vectors, `#["b" "a"] | join ","`
   answering `"a,b"`, an answer of the vector's label brought back as a
   set.
   The rules it holds: [QSDSP#residence], [QSORD#set]. ~|

:set
  |~~ A set is a vector in the one order without duplicates, `#[1 2 3]`:
      the answer of `toSet`, the set of the keys of a map `keys | toSet`,
      and the value to ask whether it
      holds an element, `#[:tea :cake] | has :tea` answering `true`. Its order is
      the one order of values, never the order written, `#[3 1 3]`
      answering `#[1 3]`.

      It is written as a vector after a hash, `#[…]`, and `::set[3 1]`
      is `#[1 3]`; a set written in braces, `#{…}`, is refused with the
      brackets it takes. It reads as a vector wherever a vector is read,
      `/0` its least element. JSON writes it as an array, which reads
      back as a vector, and sets rank after vectors and before maps.

      A set answers the verbs of vectors in its one order, `first` its
      least element and `take 2` its least two, and an answer those verbs
      give as a vector comes back a set, `#[1 2 3] | filter (gt 1)`
      answering `#[2 3]`, while `map` answers the vector of the images.
      Its own verbs are those whose answer differs: `sort`, `reverse` and
      `zip` answer a vector, which `toSet` makes a set again, `toVec` answers
      the vector of its elements, `toSet` and `distinct` answer the set
      itself, each of its elements in it once already, `groupBy` files into sets,
      and `union`, `inter` and `minus` combine sets.
      ::snippet~(::set | manifest) lists its own verbs and refusals, and
      ::snippet~(::vec | manifest) the verbs of vectors it answers.
      ::link~(::vec) ::link~(::qlang/values)
  ~~|
  ::kind
    | accepts
        |~~ A vector, built into the set of its elements in the one order
            without duplicates.
        ~~|
        ::vec
    | throws :PayloadKindNotTakenError
        |~~ `::set` builds a set from a vector, `::set[3 1 3]`; a payload
            of another kind is refused, a vector under another tag among
            them, which `payload` opens, `::Scores[3 1] | payload | tag ::set`.
        ~~|
    | proves :dropsDuplicates
        |~~ A set literal drops duplicates. ~~|
        ~("#[3 1 3]" | parse | answer | eq #[1 3])
    | proves :buildsFromAVector
        |~~ A vector under `::set` is its distinct elements. ~~|
        ~([3 1 3] | tag ::set | eq ([3 1 3] | toSet))
    | proves :reversesIntoAVector
        |~~ A reversed set is a vector. ~~|
        ~(#[3 1 2] | reverse | eq [3 2 1])
    | proves :refusesBraces
        |~~ `#{1}` does not parse, and its message names `#[`. ~~|
        ~("#{1}" | parse !| message | contains "#[")
    | proves :PayloadKindNotTakenError/refusesAMap
        |~~ A map payload is refused. ~~|
        ~(::set{:tea 4} !| get [tag (found | tag)] | eq [::set/PayloadKindNotTakenError ::map])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:set/distinct
  |~~ Answers the set itself, `it`, under the tags it came with, each of
      its elements in it once already, `::Box#[1 2] | distinct` answering
      `::Box#[1 2]`; the `distinct` of a vector keeps its elements in the
      order of their first occurrence.
      ::link~(::set/toSet) ::link~(::vec/distinct)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The set itself. ~~| ::set
    | proves :answersItself
        |~~ A set is its distinct elements already, under the tags it came
            with.
        ~~|
        ~(::Box#[1 2] | get [it distinct] | eq [::Box#[1 2] ::Box#[1 2]])
    | impl ~(it);

:set/groupBy
  |~~ Answers the map of the elements filed under the keyword their key
      answers, each group a set:
      `#[1 2 3 4] | groupBy (if (gt 2) (:big) (:small))` answers
      `{:small #[1 2] :big #[3 4]}`, where a vector files into vectors,
      each element as often as it was written. Without a key an element
      files under itself. A key that answers a string or a number files
      under the keyword of its text, `#[1 2] | groupBy` answering
      `{:"1" #[1] :"2" #[2]}`, a boolean or null under the keyword of its
      literal and a tag name under that of its name, and two values whose
      text is one meet and are refused; a key of another kind is refused
      at its element. The key is code, a quote run on each element, or
      null: `groupBy /dept`, where `groupBy (get /dept)` is refused under the
      input.
      ::link~(::vec/groupBy) ::link~(::vec/indexBy)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :key
        |~~ The code each element is filed by, null when the call gives
            none.
        ~~|
        ~(query /0) #[::null ::path ::quote]
    | throws :KeyKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The key is a path, a quote or null: written `~(get /dept)`, it
            runs on each element, while `(get /dept)` is computed once at the call,
            against the set, and refused with what it answered.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The elements filed under the keyword their key answered, each
            group a set.
        ~~|
        ::map
    | throws :KeyAnswerKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The key `groupBy` files an element under is a keyword, or a
            value `toKeyword` names one for: a string or a number files
            under the keyword of its text, a boolean or null under that of
            its literal and a tag name under that of its name. A key of
            another kind, a vector, a set, a map or a quote, is refused,
            the path of its frame naming the element and `:expected` the
            kinds a key files under.
        ~~|
    | throws :KeyTakenError
        |~~ Two values the key answers file under one keyword, `1` and
            `"1"` both under `:"1"`, and a map holds the elements of one
            value under a key: the second is refused, `key | answer` the
            keyword, `:taken` the value that filed under it first and
            the path of its frame the element that met it. A key that
            means both answers one kind,
            `~(get /id | if (tag | eq ::string) it print)`.
        ~~|
        ::vec/groupBy/KeyTakenError
    | proves :filesIntoSets
        |~~ The elements file into sets under the key they answer. ~~|
        ~(#[1 2 3] | groupBy (if (gt 1) (:big) (:small)) | get /big | eq #[2 3])
    | proves :filesOnceWhatTheVectorRepeats
        |~~ A vector files an element as often as it was written, its set
            once.
        ~~|
        ~([1 1] | get [groupBy (toSet | groupBy)] | eq [{:"1" [1 1]} {:"1" #[1]}])
    | proves :filesUnderItselfWithoutAKey
        |~~ Without a key an element files under itself. ~~|
        ~(#[:tea :cake] | groupBy | eq {:cake #[:cake] :tea #[:tea]})
    | proves :filesANumberUnderItsText
        |~~ A number files under the keyword of its text. ~~|
        ~(#[1 2] | groupBy | keys | eq [:"1" :"2"])
    | proves :filesABooleanUnderItsLiteral
        |~~ A boolean files under the keyword of its literal. ~~|
        ~(#[true false] | groupBy | eq {:false #[false] :true #[true]})
    | proves :filesNullUnderItsLiteral
        |~~ A null files under `:null`. ~~|
        ~(#[null 1] | groupBy | keys | eq [:null :"1"])
    | proves :filesATagUnderItsName
        |~~ A tag name key files under the keyword of its name. ~~|
        ~(#[1 "a"] | groupBy tag | eq {:number #[1] :string #["a"]})
    | proves :KeyTakenError/refusesTwoValuesOfOneText
        |~~ A number and a string of one text meet under one key. ~~|
        ~(#[1 "1"] | groupBy !| get [tag (key | answer) taken] | eq [::set/groupBy/KeyTakenError :"1" 1])
    | proves :KeyAnswerKindNotTakenError/refusesAVectorKey
        |~~ An element that is a vector files under no key, and is refused
            with the kinds a key files under.
        ~~|
        ~(#[[1]] | groupBy !| get [tag (trace | last | path) (key | expected)] | eq [::set/groupBy/KeyAnswerKindNotTakenError /0 #[::boolean ::keyword ::null ::number ::string ::tag]])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:set/inter
  |~~ Answers the set of the elements both sets hold, under the tags the
      two share: `#[:tea :cake :jam] | inter #[:cake :pie]` answers
      `#[:cake]`, and
      whether the set holds each of several elements is
      `inter #[1 2] | eq #[1 2]`. The other value is a set the call
      gives; a vector or a map beside a set is refused under the input,
      and so is a call that gives none.
      ::link~(::set/union) ::link~(::set/minus)
      ::link~(::vec/inter) ::link~(::vec/has)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :other |~~ The set whose elements stay, which the call gives. ~~| /0 ::set
    | throws :OtherKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The other value is a set; a vector is made a set by `toSet`
            first, `#[1 2] | inter ([2] | toSet)`.
        ~~|
    | throws :OtherNotGivenError
        |~~ `inter` keeps of the subject what the set the call gives holds,
            `#[1 2] | inter #[2]`; what the sets a set holds share is the
            verb of vectors, `#[#[1 2] #[2 3]] | vec/inter`.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The elements both sets hold, a set under the tags they share.
        ~~|
        ::set
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `inter` takes one set: several cross one after another,
            `inter #[1 2] | inter #[2 3]`.
        ~~|
    | proves :keepsTheCommonElements
        |~~ The elements both hold stay. ~~|
        ~(#[:tea :cake :jam] | inter #[:cake :pie] | eq #[:cake])
    | proves :asksForSeveralElements
        |~~ A set holds each of several when crossing with them keeps them
            all.
        ~~|
        ~(#[1 2 3] | inter #[1 2] | eq #[1 2])
    | proves :OtherKindNotTakenError/refusesAVector
        |~~ A vector beside a set is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(#[:tea] | inter [:tea] !| tag | eq ::set/inter/OtherKindNotTakenError)
    | proves :OtherNotGivenError/refusesACallWithNoSet
        |~~ A call that gives no set is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(#[#[1 2] #[2 3]] | inter !| tag | eq ::set/inter/OtherNotGivenError)
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesTwoSets
        |~~ A call with two sets is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~(#[1 2] | inter #[1] #[2] !| tag | eq ::set/inter/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{};

:set/minus
  |~~ Answers the set of the elements of the subject the other set does
      not hold, under the tags the two share:
      `#[:tea :cake :jam] | minus #[:cake]` answers `#[:jam :tea]`. The other value is a set the call gives; a
      vector or a map beside a set is refused under the input, and so is
      a call that gives none. `union` and `inter` answer the whole and the
      common part.
      ::link~(::set/union) ::link~(::set/inter)
      ::link~(::vec/minus)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :other |~~ The set whose elements go, which the call gives. ~~| /0 ::set
    | throws :OtherKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The value taken from a set is a set; a vector is made a set by
            `toSet` first, `#[1 2] | minus ([2] | toSet)`.
        ~~|
    | throws :OtherNotGivenError
        |~~ `minus` takes from the subject the set the call gives,
            `#[1 2] | minus #[2]`; the sets a set holds meet in its one
            order as the vector it is, `toVec | minus`.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The elements the other set does not hold, a set under the tags
            of the subject.
        ~~|
        ::set
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `minus` takes one set: several go one after another,
            `minus #[2] | minus #[3]`.
        ~~|
    | proves :removesTheOtherElements
        |~~ The elements of the other set go. ~~|
        ~(#[:tea :cake :jam] | minus #[:cake] | eq #[:jam :tea])
    | proves :takesSeveralOneAfterAnother
        |~~ Several sets go one after another. ~~|
        ~(#[1 2 3] | minus #[2] | minus #[3] | eq #[1])
    | proves :OtherKindNotTakenError/refusesAMap
        |~~ A map beside a set is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(#[:tea] | minus {:tea 4} !| tag | eq ::set/minus/OtherKindNotTakenError)
    | proves :OtherNotGivenError/refusesACallWithNoSet
        |~~ A call that gives no set is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(#[#[1] #[2]] | minus !| tag | eq ::set/minus/OtherNotGivenError)
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesTwoSets
        |~~ A call with two sets is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~(#[1 2 3] | minus #[2] #[3] !| tag | eq ::set/minus/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{};

:set/reverse
  |~~ Answers the vector of the elements from the greatest down,
      `#[:tea :cake :jam] | reverse` answering `[:tea :jam :cake]`, where
      a vector reverses the order it was written in, `[3 1 2] | reverse`
      answering `[2 1 3]`. A set keeps no order but the one order, so the
      answer is a vector, and `toSet` after it gives the set back.
      ::link~(::set/sort) ::link~(::vec/reverse)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The elements from the greatest down, a vector. ~~| ::vec
    | proves :answersAVector
        |~~ The reversed elements are a vector. ~~|
        ~(#[:tea :cake :jam] | reverse | eq [:tea :jam :cake])
    | proves :reversesTheOneOrderNotTheVector
        |~~ A vector reverses its order as written, its set the one order.
        ~~|
        ~([3 1 2] | get [reverse (toSet | reverse)] | eq [[2 1 3] [3 2 1]])
    | proves :answersAVectorThatToSetMakesASet
        |~~ A reversed set is a set again after `toSet`. ~~|
        ~(#[3 1 2] | reverse | toSet | eq #[1 2 3])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:set/sort
  |~~ Answers the vector of the elements in the order the key ranks them,
      the one order without a key: `#[{:age 41} {:age 30}] | sort /age`
      answers `[{:age 30} {:age 41}]`, and a key under `desc` ranks from the
      greatest down, `#[1 2 3] | sort desc` answering `[3 2 1]`. A set
      keeps no order but the one order, so the answer is a vector, and
      `toSet` after it brings the set back in the one order. The key is
      code, a quote run on each element with the element as its subject;
      a value computed at the call instead is refused under the
      input.
      ::link~(::vec/sort) ::link~(::set/reverse)
      ::link~(::desc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :key
        |~~ The code each element is ranked by, null when the call gives
            none.
        ~~|
        ~(query /0) #[::null ::path ::quote]
    | throws :KeyKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The key is a path, a quote or null: written `~(get /age)`, it runs
            on each element, while `(get /age)` is computed once at the call,
            against the set, and refused with what it answered.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The elements in the order the key ranks them, a vector. ~~|
        ::vec
    | proves :answersAVector
        |~~ The sorted elements are a vector. ~~|
        ~(#[:tea :cake :jam] | sort | eq [:cake :jam :tea])
    | proves :ranksByTheKey
        |~~ A key ranks the elements by what it answers. ~~|
        ~(#[{:age 41} {:age 30}] | sort /age | eq [{:age 30} {:age 41}])
    | proves :descendsByADescendingKey
        |~~ A key under `desc` ranks from the greatest down. ~~|
        ~(#[1 2 3] | sort desc | eq [3 2 1])
    | proves :losesItsOrderToToSet
        |~~ `toSet` after a sort brings the set back in the one order. ~~|
        ~(#[1 2 3] | sort desc | toSet | eq #[1 2 3])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:set/toSet
  |~~ Answers the set itself, `it`, under the tags it came with,
      `::Box#[1 2] | toSet` answering `::Box#[1 2]`, so `map toSet`
      turns vectors and sets into sets; on a set `distinct` answers it
      too.
      ::link~(::vec/toSet) ::link~(::set/distinct)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The set itself. ~~| ::set
    | proves :answersItself
        |~~ A set answers itself. ~~|
        ~(#[1 2] | toSet | eq #[1 2])
    | proves :keepsTheTagsItCameWith
        |~~ A set under a tag answers itself under the tag. ~~|
        ~(::Box#[1 2] | toSet | eq ::Box#[1 2])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:set/toVec
  |~~ Answers the vector of the set's elements in its order,
      `#[2 1] | toVec` answering `[1 2]`, a vector whose verbs keep it a
      vector, `#[3 1 2] | toVec | take 2` answering `[1 2]` where the set
      takes the set `#[1 2]`. `toSet` turns the vector back into the set.
      A map has no `toVec`: its pairs are `entries`, its keys `keys` and
      its values `vals`; and the characters of a string are `split ""`.
      ::link~(::vec/toSet) ::link~(::map/entries)
      ::link~(::map/keys) ::link~(::map/vals)
      ::link~(::string/split)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The vector of the elements. ~~| ::vec
    | proves :leadsAMapToItsParts
        |~~ A map is led to its pairs and its values, the verbs that
            answer a vector, and a string to `split`.
        ~~|
        ~(get [({:id 7} | toVec !| nearest) ("tea" | toVec !| nearest)] | eq [[:entries :keys :vals] [:split]])
    | proves :keepsTheVerbsOfVectorsOnAVector
        |~~ The vector of a set takes as a vector, the set as a set. ~~|
        ~(#[3 1 2] | get [(toVec | take 2) (take 2)] | eq [[1 2] #[1 2]])
    | proves :readsBack
        |~~ A set turned into a vector turns back into itself. ~~|
        ~(#[2 1] | toVec | get [it toSet] | eq [[1 2] #[1 2]])
    | impl ::builtin{};

:set/union
  |~~ Answers the set of the elements of either set, in the one order and
      under the tags the two share: `#[:tea :cake] | union #[:cake :jam]`
      answers `#[:cake :jam :tea]`. The other value is a set the call
      gives; a vector or a map beside a set is refused under the input,
      and so is a call that gives none, and the parts a call computes
      against one subject unite by `unionOf`. `minus` and `inter` answer
      the difference and the common part.
      ::link~(::set/minus) ::link~(::set/inter)
      ::link~(::vec/union) ::link~(::any/unionOf)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :other |~~ The other set, which the call gives. ~~| /0 ::set
    | throws :OtherKindNotTakenError
        |~~ The other value is a set; a vector is made a set by `toSet`
            first, `#[1] | union ([2 3] | toSet)`.
        ~~|
    | throws :OtherNotGivenError
        |~~ `union` unites the subject with the set the call gives,
            `#[1] | union #[2]`; the sets a set holds unite by the verb of
            vectors, `#[#[1] #[2]] | vec/union`, or by `flat`.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The elements of both sets, a set under the tags they share.
        ~~|
        ::set
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `union` unites the subject with one set: several unite one
            after another, `union #[2] | union #[3]`, or as the vector of
            them, `[#[1] #[2] #[3]] | union`.
        ~~|
    | proves :mergesTwoSets
        |~~ The union holds the elements of either. ~~|
        ~(#[:tea :cake] | union #[:cake :jam] | eq #[:cake :jam :tea])
    | proves :unitesSeveralOneAfterAnother
        |~~ Several sets unite one after another. ~~|
        ~(#[1] | union #[2] | union #[3] | eq #[1 2 3])
    | proves :OtherKindNotTakenError/refusesAVector
        |~~ A vector beside a set is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(#[:tea] | union [:cake] !| tag | eq ::set/union/OtherKindNotTakenError)
    | proves :OtherNotGivenError/refusesACallWithNoSet
        |~~ A call that gives no set is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(#[#[1] #[2]] | union !| tag | eq ::set/union/OtherNotGivenError)
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesTwoSets
        |~~ A call with two sets is refused under the verb. ~~|
        ~(#[1] | union #[2] #[3] !| tag | eq ::set/union/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{};

:set/zip
  |~~ Answers the vectors the set holds read side by side, in its one
      order, one vector of the elements at each index, as long as the
      shortest: `#[[1 1] [2 2]] | zip` answers `[[1 2] [1 2]]`, a vector,
      since two columns may be equal, as `sort` and `reverse` answer one.
      It takes no value.
      ::link~(::vec/zip) ::link~(::set/reverse)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The elements at each index, a vector each. ~~| ::vec
    | throws :ElementKindNotTakenError
        |~~ `zip` reads the vectors a set holds side by side; the path of
            its frame names the first element that is no vector, the
            vectors its elements hold read first, `map /scores | zip`.
        ~~|
    | proves :keepsEqualColumns
        |~~ The columns stand in a vector, two equal ones kept. ~~|
        ~(#[[1 1] [2 2]] | zip | eq [[1 2] [1 2]])
    | proves :ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ An element that is no vector is refused at its index. ~~|
        ~(#[1 [2]] | zip !| get [tag (trace | last | path) (subject | expected)] | eq [::set/zip/ElementKindNotTakenError /0 #[::vec]])
    | proves :ElementKindNotTakenError/readsTheVectorsOfItsElementsFirst
        |~~ The vectors the elements of a set hold are read first. ~~|
        ~(#[{:scores [1 2]} {:scores [3 4]}] | map /scores | zip | eq [[1 3] [2 4]])
    | impl ::builtin{};
